                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Piercing of a boson star by a black hole\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"13 pages, 11 figures, 2 movies in ancillary files. \
revised to match\\n  the published version. Movies are also available on\\n  \
https://centra.tecnico.ulisboa.pt/network/grit/files/movies/\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Phys. Rev. D 106, 044030 (2022)\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.106.044030\""}]}, {
               
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"gr-qc astro-ph.HE hep-ph hep-th\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]}\
, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  New light fundamental fields are natural candidates \
for all or a fraction of\\ndark matter. Self-gravitating structures of such \
fields might be common objects\\nin the universe, and could comprise even \
galactic halos. These structures would\\ninteract gravitationally with black \
holes, a process of the utmost importance\\nsince it dictates their lifetime, \
the black hole motion, and possible\\ngravitational radiation emission.\\n  \
Here, we study the dynamics of a black hole piercing through a much \
larger\\nfully relativistic boson star, made of a complex minimally coupled \
massive\\nscalar without self-interactions. As the black hole pierces through \
the bosonic\\nstructure, it is slowed down by accretion and dynamical \
friction, giving rise\\nto gravitational-wave emission. Since we are \
interested in studying the\\ninteraction with large and heavy scalar \
structures, we consider mass ratios up\\nto $q\\\\sim 10$ and length ratios \
${\\\\cal L} \\\\sim 62$.\\n  Somewhat surprisingly, for all our simulations, \
the black hole accretes more\\nthan 95% of the boson star material, even if \
an initially small black hole\\ncollides with large velocity. This is a \
consequence of an extreme \\\"tidal\\ncapture\\\" process, which binds the \
black hole and the boson star together, for\\nthese mass ratios. We find \
evidence of a \\\"gravitational atom\\\" left behind as a\\nproduct of the \
process.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
