           TableForm[{
            "id" -> "2206.00021", "submitter" -> "Zhen Zhong", "authors" -> 
             "Vitor Cardoso, Taishi Ikeda, Zhen Zhong and Miguel Zilh\\~ao", 
             "title" -> "Piercing of a boson star by a black hole", 
             "comments" -> 
             "13 pages, 11 figures, 2 movies in ancillary files. revised to \
match\n  the published version. Movies are also available on\n  \
https://centra.tecnico.ulisboa.pt/network/grit/files/movies/", "journal-ref" -> 
             "Phys. Rev. D 106, 044030 (2022)", "doi" -> 
             "10.1103/PhysRevD.106.044030", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
             "gr-qc astro-ph.HE hep-ph hep-th", "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  New light fundamental fields are natural candidates for all \
or a fraction of\ndark matter. Self-gravitating structures of such fields \
might be common objects\nin the universe, and could comprise even galactic \
halos. These structures would\ninteract gravitationally with black holes, a \
process of the utmost importance\nsince it dictates their lifetime, the black \
hole motion, and possible\ngravitational radiation emission.\n  Here, we \
study the dynamics of a black hole piercing through a much larger\nfully \
relativistic boson star, made of a complex minimally coupled massive\nscalar \
without self-interactions. As the black hole pierces through the bosonic\n\
structure, it is slowed down by accretion and dynamical friction, giving rise\
\nto gravitational-wave emission. Since we are interested in studying the\n\
interaction with large and heavy scalar structures, we consider mass ratios \
up\nto $q\\sim 10$ and length ratios ${\\cal L} \\sim 62$.\n  Somewhat \
surprisingly, for all our simulations, the black hole accretes more\nthan 95% \
of the boson star material, even if an initially small black hole\ncollides \
with large velocity. This is a consequence of an extreme \"tidal\ncapture\" \
process, which binds the black hole and the boson star together, for\nthese \
mass ratios. We find evidence of a \"gravitational atom\" left behind as a\n\
product of the process.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
               "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
                "Tue, 31 May 2022 18:00:05 GMT"}, {
               "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
                "Tue, 30 Aug 2022 15:06:45 GMT"}}, "update_date" -> 
             "2022-08-31", 
