                RowBox[{"\"submitter\"", "\[Rule]", "\"Paolo Pani\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"authors\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Paolo Pani, Vitor Cardoso\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"title\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Are black holes in alternative theories serious \
astrophysical\\n  candidates? The case for Einstein-Dilaton-Gauss-Bonnet \
black holes\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"comments\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"17 pages - v1: references added - v2: Minor \
corrections\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"journal-ref\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"Phys.Rev.D79:084031,2009\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"doi\"", "\[Rule]", "\"10.1103/PhysRevD.79.084031\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"report-no\"", "\[Rule]", "Null"}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"categories\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"gr-qc astro-ph.HE hep-th\""}]}, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"license\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/\""}]}\
, {
                
                RowBox[{
                 "\"abstract\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  "\"  It is generally accepted that Einstein's theory will \
get some as yet unknown\\ncorrections, possibly large in the strong field \
regime. An ideal place to look\\nfor these modifications is around the \
vicinities of compact objects such as\\nblack holes. Our case study here are \
Dilatonic Black Holes, which arise in the\\nframework of Gauss-Bonnet \
couplings and one-loop corrected four-dimensional\\neffective theory of \
heterotic superstrings at low energies. These are\\ninteresting objects as a \
prototype for alternative, yet well-behaved gravity\\ntheories: they evade \
the \\\"no-hair\\\" theorem of General Relativity but were\\nproved to be \
stable against radial perturbations. We investigate the viability\\nof these \
black holes as astrophysical objects and try to provide some means \
to\\ndistinguish them from black holes in General Relativity. We start by \
extending\\nprevious works and establishing the stability of these black \
holes against\\naxial perturbations. We then look for solutions of the field \
equations\\ndescribing slowly rotating black holes and study geodesic motion \
around this\\ngeometry. Depending on the values of mass, dilaton charge and \
angular momentum\\nof the solution, one can have measurable differences in \
the ISCO location and\\norbital frequency, relatively to black holes in \
General Relativity. Such\\ndifferences may be useful in future experiments, \
to discriminate between\\nalternative theories of gravity.\\n\""}]}, {
                RowBox[{"\"versions\"", "\[Rule]", 
                  RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"{", 
                    RowBox[{
                    RowBox[{"\"version\"", "\[Rule]", "\"v1\""}], ",", 
                    
