          Annotation[#, 
           
           TableForm[{
            "id" -> "0902.1569", "submitter" -> "Paolo Pani", "authors" -> 
             "Paolo Pani, Vitor Cardoso", "title" -> 
             "Are black holes in alternative theories serious astrophysical\n \
 candidates? The case for Einstein-Dilaton-Gauss-Bonnet black holes", 
             "comments" -> 
             "17 pages - v1: references added - v2: Minor corrections", 
             "journal-ref" -> "Phys.Rev.D79:084031,2009", "doi" -> 
             "10.1103/PhysRevD.79.084031", "report-no" -> Null, "categories" -> 
             "gr-qc astro-ph.HE hep-th", "license" -> 
             "http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/", 
             "abstract" -> 
             "  It is generally accepted that Einstein's theory will get some \
as yet unknown\ncorrections, possibly large in the strong field regime. An \
ideal place to look\nfor these modifications is around the vicinities of \
compact objects such as\nblack holes. Our case study here are Dilatonic Black \
Holes, which arise in the\nframework of Gauss-Bonnet couplings and one-loop \
corrected four-dimensional\neffective theory of heterotic superstrings at low \
energies. These are\ninteresting objects as a prototype for alternative, yet \
well-behaved gravity\ntheories: they evade the \"no-hair\" theorem of General \
Relativity but were\nproved to be stable against radial perturbations. We \
investigate the viability\nof these black holes as astrophysical objects and \
try to provide some means to\ndistinguish them from black holes in General \
Relativity. We start by extending\nprevious works and establishing the \
stability of these black holes against\naxial perturbations. We then look for \
solutions of the field equations\ndescribing slowly rotating black holes and \
study geodesic motion around this\ngeometry. Depending on the values of mass, \
dilaton charge and angular momentum\nof the solution, one can have measurable \
differences in the ISCO location and\norbital frequency, relatively to black \
holes in General Relativity. Such\ndifferences may be useful in future \
experiments, to discriminate between\nalternative theories of gravity.\n", 
             "versions" -> {{
               "version" -> "v1", "created" -> 
                "Tue, 10 Feb 2009 01:26:25 GMT"}, {
               "version" -> "v2", "created" -> 
